{"ts":1781440816,"op":"assert","clause":"rule(plugin_mailbox_no_reentrant_host_fn, architecture, 'No host function exposed to WASM guests may invoke a path that reaches PluginHostPort::on_file_indexed/on_file_changed: the worker thread is the single consumer of its own bounded mailbox, so re-entering fan_out from inside a hook handler would block the worker on a send to its own full mailbox and self-deadlock. Today this holds only because all host functions (host_read_file, host_list_files, ast_store_get/put, host_log) are read-only or non-reentrant; adding any file-mutating host function reintroduces the hazard.', high, 'feat/internal-plugin-queue design+review 2026-06')"}
{"ts":1781440817,"op":"assert","clause":"trigger(plugin_mailbox_no_reentrant_host_fn, 'crates/core_engine/src/adapters/plugin/loader.rs', directory)"}
{"ts":1781440820,"op":"assert","clause":"rule(plugin_registry_no_write_on_serving_path, architecture, 'No MCP-serving code path may take a write() guard on the Arc<RwLock<PluginHostRegistry>>. Watcher hook delivery (SharedPluginHost::on_file_*) does a blocking backpressuring mailbox send while holding the registry RwLock read guard; this never starves MCP only because every hot-path consumer (declared_tools, call_instance_tool, hooks) is also a reader and RwLock permits concurrent readers. A write() on a serving path would be blocked by watcher backpressure. register() is the only writer and runs at startup before the Arc<RwLock> is shared.', high, 'feat/internal-plugin-queue design+review 2026-06')"}
{"ts":1781440821,"op":"assert","clause":"trigger(plugin_registry_no_write_on_serving_path, 'crates/core_engine/src/domain/plugin_host', directory)"}
